==English==
{{rfv|en}}
===Etymology===
Originates from the title of the British [[sitcom]] ''Drop the Dead Donkey''. ''see'' [[Wikipedia:Drop the Dead Donkey|Wikipedia entry]].

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|journalism}} A [[news]] item of no real significance, usually of whimsical or sentimental nature, placed at the end of a news bulletin or in a [[newspaper]] as filler. A dead donkey can often be removed from the programme or publication if a more significant story needs extra time or space.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1994 |title=New Scientist - Volume 141, Issues 1907-1912 |author=|page=3 |ISBN=|passage=Swap these ages around to produce a 59-year-old father and a 45-year-old mother and what was previously a hot news story becomes a '''dead donkey'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003  |title=Of corpse: death and humor in folklore and popular culture |author=Peter Narváez |page=25 |ISBN=|passage=When disaster strikes, the '''dead donkey''' does not get dropped, but merely occurs later on the television news.}}

